  5    knock-outs (rocprofv3 kernel trace, I=384 T=32k, v5 cell): full 617 us, noepi 235, nodmaB 636,
       nodmaA+nodmaB 532; s2nt off: 475 -> 651 us (serial)                         -> the y_rows stores bind
verdict: SHIP BM2=64 NW2=4 pipe2=hybrid2 D2=2 diag2=wpe3+s2nt+agN at I=768 T=512..4096 (ag64; ag32 at 4096),
  BM2=32 NW2=2 (ag32) at I=768 T=256, and at I=1536 T=256 (ag32) / 512 / 1024 (ag64). No change at I=384.
binding limit (I=384, T >= 8192): the y_rows write. Stage 2 writes R*H*2 B (T=32768: 2.01 GB, 385 us at the
  measured 5.23 TB/s row-write rate) plus 0.16 GB of W2 / h reads: floor ~415 us vs 475 us measured (87%;
  T=8192: 125 vs 131 us, 95%); the combine (455 us) reads the same 2.01 GB and writes 0.4 GB at ~5.3 TB/s.
  Fusing them (FC) keeps the round trip (iteration 1); removing it needs atomics or lossy rows (excluded).

== S2-B  persistent stage 2 on the non-il4 pipes (next-tile prefetch before the stores) ==
logs: r5_s2b_pers.log; traces att_r5/s2b768 (I=768 T=8192 BM64 non-persistent), att_r5/s2bpf (PF, per tile)
iterations (BM64 hybrid2 wpe3+s2nt+ag64 = the S2-A arm as reference; s2 us, serial GPU 0):
  1    plain PERS2=1/2/3 (grid = CUs x PERS, tile loop)                     -> -39 .. -150%: 36 spills (LICM hoists
                                                                               the n_rows-derived offsets out of the
                                                                               tile loop)
  2    per-tile opaque copies (as IL4P) for every PERS build: 0 spills      -> 0 .. -4% vs non-persistent
  3    trace (non-persistent, I=768 T=8192): per CTA prologue 6.7k / loop 9.2k (16.8% MFMA duty, gaps behind
       the B register loads) / epilogue 3.7k cycles; 72 CTAs per CU in ~24 rounds of 3, HBM ~3.6 TB/s ->
       per-CTA latency bound. pf: tile w+1's A DMAs into the ring during tile w's epilogue (row staging moved
       after the ring), issued after the router-weight loads (vmcnt in order). 3 waves/SIMD: 8-13 spills,
       98-117 us vs 93; 2 waves/SIMD (wpe2): 91.7-92.1 vs 92.5 (T=2048), 245.7 vs 235.6 (T=8192)
                                                                            -> prefetch = one more wave, not both
  4    pfb: also tile w+1's first DB steps of B registers, carried in the tile state (2 waves, 0 spills)
                                                                            -> 91.5 vs 93.1 (T=2048), 254.7 vs 237.7
                                                                               (T=8192), I=1536 T=4096 -4.9%
  5    async2 + pf (B through LDS, so the prefetch is register-free)        -> LDS 98-120 KB, 1 CTA/CU:
                                                                               -2.5 .. -46%
  6    pfl: next-tile DMAs after the row staging (no hoisted weights): 3 waves, 5-9 spills
                                                                            -> 98.3 vs 93.4 (T=2048), 267.6 vs 236.2
  7    I=384 T=32768 (store-bound, S2-A): PERS2=2/3 -1.1 / -2.3%, async2+pf -19 .. -24%
verdict: REJECT persistent stage 2 on the non-il4 pipes (no cell gains > 1.5% over the non-persistent S2-A /
  v5 configs). Kept as additive diags (pf, pfb, pfl; opaque copies for every PERS build); correctness at
  T=4097 (test_options) and T=40000 (test_large) OK, bit-identical to the non-persistent kernel.
binding limit (I=768 T=2048..8192, BM64 hybrid2): per-CTA latency of the B global->register loads (1.5k cycles
  per K step vs 256 MFMA cycles). It is hidden either by a third wave per SIMD (VGPR budget 168) or by
  cross-tile prefetch state (A ring + staging LDS, B registers), not both: with the prefetch the third wave
  spills (iterations 3, 6) and with B in LDS the ring caps residency at 1 CTA/CU (5). At I=384 the y_rows
  stores bind (S2-A).

== S1-D  split-K stage 1 for the < 4 CU-wave cells (fixed-order reduction, last-arriver epilogue) ==
logs: r5_s1d_sk.log; bench/wave_eff.py (tile counts); traces att_r5/s1dsk1, s1dsk2 (I=384 T=1024)
setup: diag skN (stage 1, async / async2 / hybrid / hybrid2 / pingpong): work unit = (tile, k-range of
  KS/N steps); resource bases start at the unit's first K step; each unit stores its fp32 partial tile to a
  workspace and bumps a per-tile counter (agent-scope atomic); the last arriver sums the N partials in
  fixed k order (deterministic; its own from registers) and runs the SwiGLU/quant epilogue, re-arming the
  counter; the others run the epilogue with nrows = 0 (all stores masked, partial loads OOB = no traffic).
  wave_eff.py (uniform routing): 0.84 wave efficiency at every candidate cell, but row fill 0.56.
iterations (stage-1 us, serial GPU 0):
  1    agent-scope release/acquire fences around the counter (cached partials) -> I=384 T=1024: 68.8 -> 232 (sk2),
       410 (sk4); I=1536 T=2048 271 -> 1038: each fence writes back / invalidates the whole per-XCD L2
  2    partials write-through (sc0 sc1) + vmcnt(0) drain, no fences           -> 84.5 (sk2), 108.5 (sk4): -23 / -58%
  3    last arriver reuses its own partial from registers (1/N fewer reads)
  4    small T (BM16/32 async), warm and flushed (new s1_sweep --flush):        -> flushed I=384 T=32 -0.1 .. +1.0%;
       every other cell -2 .. -56% (I=768 / 1536 T=32 / 256, I=384 T=256)
  5    trace I=384 T=1024 (hybrid2 BM64): unsplit wave 48 steps in 95.9k cycles, 72k of it behind the B
       register loads (1.5k / step); sk2 wave 24 steps in 83.9k (3.5k / step): twice the units in flight
       raise the per-step load latency 2.3x (Little's law at the HBM limit)  -> no throughput to gain
  6    pingpong 256x256 cells (256 KB partials): I=384 T=4096 / 8192, I=768 T=4096 sk2/3/4 -> -58 .. -175%
verdict: REJECT split-K / stream-K for stage 1 (kept as diag skN / skf / sknt; T=4097 and T=40000 tests OK).
  The idle-slot fraction (wave efficiency 0.84) does not cost time in these cells.
binding limit (stage 1, T <= 8192): W13 weight streaming at the HBM limit (warm: 80-90% of the W13-read
  floor at T <= 1024). Extra units only add bytes in flight (higher per-step latency, same throughput) plus
  fp32 partial traffic (BM*BN*4 per unit and split) and duplicated prologues. Flushed small T is
  latency-bound below the floor (I=384 T=32: 95 us for ~170 MB of touched weights) but not CTA-limited:
  more units do not help there either (iteration 4) -> ST-1.

== ST-1  small-T deep prefetch / CTA shape (flushed and warm) ==
logs: r5_st1.log; bench/tlb_probe.py; compare.flush now has FLYMOE_FLUSH=read (clean flush)
iterations (serial GPU 0, us vs the v5 table cell in-run):
  1    write flush (compare.flush: fill_ of 512 MB), stage 1 BM16/32 NW1/2 async D6/D8, async2 D6, hybrid2 D4
       at I=384 / 1536 T=32 / 256                                            -> -2.8 .. +3.8% s1 (best hybrid2 D4)
  2    flushed penalty ~50 us per stage at every width / T (I=384 T=32 s1 48 -> 99, I=1536 T=32 153 -> 209):
       constant, not proportional to touched bytes -> not in-flight depth. Touching one dword per 4 KB page of
       W13/W2 after the flush (TLB warm, data cold): -0..-8 us                -> TLB not binding
  3    read flush (sum over the same 512 MB, caches end holding clean lines): s1 55.3 / s2 35.7 at I=384 T=32
       (warm 48.2 / 30.7), 152.8 / 86.0 at I=1536 T=32 (warm 153.6 / 84.5)   -> the write-flush penalty is the
       write-back of the dirty MALL lines the flush leaves, paid by the next kernel's reads (benchmark artifact)
  4    clean-flushed and warm sweep, stage 1 hybrid2 BM16 NW1 D4/D6 at T=32..256, all widths:
         I=384 T=32 +3.3 / +5.1% (warm / clean flush), I=768 T=32 +7.4 / +7.7, T=64 +2.3 / +2.1,
         I=1536 T=32 +3.6 / +3.6, T=64 +1.8 / +2.0; T >= 128 -0.4 .. +2.0; D6 never better than D4
  5    stage 2 BM16 NW1 nos2w, hybrid2 D2 / D3 and async D3 at T=32..256, all widths (s2+cb):
         I=384 hybrid2 +2.1 .. +6.5% at every T (both flush modes), I=768 T=32 +1.7 / +6.0 (D3), T=64 +6.1 /
         +4.3 (D3), T=256 +3.3 / +1.8 (D2), I=1536 T=32 +5.1 / +4.4 (D3), T=64 async +2.7 / +2.6;
         I=1536 T=128 / 256 and I=768 T=128 -0.6 .. -5.8
verdict: SHIP (into make_tiles_v6 via the pick sweeps, > +1.5% warm): stage 1 hybrid2 BM16 NW1 D4 at
  I=384 / 768 / 1536 T=32, I=768 T=64, I=1536 T=64; stage 2 BM16 NW1 hybrid2 nos2w (D2/D3 per cell) at
  I=384 T=32..256, I=768 T=64 / 256, I=1536 T=32, async D3 at I=1536 T=64. Deeper prefetch (D6/D8) rejected.
binding limit (T <= 256): with a clean flush the cells run within 0..15% of warm, i.e. at the warm
  weight-streaming rate (S1-D); the remaining gains come from the 16-row CTA (more CTAs in flight over the
  ~T*5/128 rows per expert), not from prefetch depth. The ~50 us per-stage flushed penalty in the round-4/5
  flushed numbers is the dirty write-back of compare.flush; the final A/B reports both flush modes.

== ST-2  fused persistent s1+s2 small-T kernel; launch-removal (dataflow) extension ==
logs: r5_st2.log; bench/st2_sweep.py (whole-forward timing, gate: h_q / h_s / out byte-equal to the table
arm over 3-4 forwards); graph-replay round-robin timing for the sub-us deltas (warm).
iterations (I=384 T=32 unless noted; serial GPU 0):
  1-4  fused s1+s2 (gemm.build_fused, FZ CTAs/CU, grid-wide counters): dynamic per-item tickets -170..-460%
       (same-address atomics serialize at ~14-20 ns), static round robin -29%, skeleton knock-outs: one exit
       counter x grid = ~14 us; per-tile re-arm + counters 4 KB apart -> break-even
  5-9  occupancy (hybrid2 D2 stage 1: 103 VGPR, 11 KB -> 14 CTAs/CU), W2-before-wait hook (no gain),
       s_memrealtime timeline (stage-2 items a 5-6 us serial chain; coherence costs nothing), cross-item PF
       prefetch 62.4 -> 58.4 us, XCD-contiguous stage-1 order (no gain)
  10-11 forward level (warm / clean flush): best fused FZ=16 pf+pfb chunk 74.8 / 84.1, best unfused pair
       (hybrid2 D1=2 + hybrid2 D2=2 nos2w) 72.7 / 81.4 -> fused s1+s2 rejected. Stage-1 items median / max
       30.6 / 34.7 us alone vs 34.5 / 40.9 with stage 2 running (both stages HBM streams); balanced stage-2
       chunks start more stage-2 work early and stretch the stage-1 tail to 45 us
  12-13 launch upper bound and atomics: 8.4k per-item atomics 4 KB apart ~2 us; the per-CTA serial poll
       latency (~18 us over a persistent CTA's items) is what made the fused waits expensive
  14   dataflow fusion, non-persistent: QF = activation quant as the first ceil(T*QC/8)*8 CTAs of the stage-1
       launch (tiles issue their weight loads, wait on per-token flags, read a_q past L2); CF = combine as T*4
       extra CTAs of the stage-2 launch. Counting flags (consumer atomics + re-arm): QF +2.0, CF +2.1 us
  15   epoch flags (prefix plan kernel bumps an int32 epoch per forward; flags only grow, no atomics; CF
       waits on exactly the (row, n-block) flags its column quarter needs): QF+CF 72.3 vs 72.9 warm,
       82.2 vs 80.6 flushed. Graph replay: host 30-40 us < GPU 71 us; a tiny-kernel boundary costs
       ~0.5-1 us of GPU time (the 3-4 us rocprof kernel durations overstate it)
  16   QF + deeper stage-1 weight prefetch (D1=3..8), graph round robin: D1=8 QF 69.7 vs best unfused 71.3;
       CF +1.3 us on top
  17   QF timeline (s_memrealtime per CTA): quant flags med 5.4 / max 6.8 us at D1=8 (2.5 / 3.6 at D1=2: the
       quant's x loads queue behind the ~38 MB weight flood); tiles released med 8.2 us because loads retire
       in order (the flag poll waits for the CTA's 8 prefetched B steps); the qfnw knock-out (no wait, wrong
       results) bounds what is left at ~2 us. QP = quant as extra CTAs of the plan's hist launch (ordered
       before stage 1 by the plan's own boundaries, no flags): -0.4..-0.9 us
  18   cross-cell (st2_sweep, warm / clean flush, us): I=384 T=32 D1=2 pair 71.5 / 82.3, +QP 70.6 / 81.2;
       I=384 T=256 D1=8 pair 113.3 / 114.2, +QP 111.5 / 111.8 (+6.4 / +5.6% vs table), +QF 118.1 / 119.0;
       I=1536 T=32 / 256: every arm within +-1 us of the D1=2 / D1=8 pairs
  19   after fixing the QF grid (quant CTAs = T*QC, was T): I=384 T=32 flushed D1=8 QF 80.5 vs D1=2 pair 82.1
       (+2.0%), warm 72.1 vs 72.1; QP at large T (clean flush): I=384 T=4096 +1.6%, T=32768 +0.9%,
       I=1536 T=4096 +1.3%, T=32768 -0.3%
verdict: fused persistent s1+s2 REJECTED (best -2..-3 us vs the best unfused pair; target >= 5% not met
  after 13 iterations). Launch removal: QP SHIPS as a per-cell measured flag in make_tiles_v6 (+0.6..2.7 us
  at I=384 / 768 small T, +1..1.6% at T=4096); QF ships only where the v6 pick sweep confirms it (I=384 T=32
  flushed +2.0%); CF never wins (rejected). All three are lossless (gated byte-equal) and covered at
  T=5 / 300 / 4097 (test_options, all widths) and T=40000 (test_large).
binding limit: at T <= 256 both GEMMs are single-wave HBM streams (stage 1 ~205 MB of W13 at I=384 T=32 in
  ~37 us), so overlapping them only shares bandwidth and each stage-2 tile still waits for the slowest of its
  NB1 stage-1 items. What fusion can remove is the boundary itself, which costs ~0.5-1 us of GPU time per
  tiny kernel under graph replay. An in-kernel dependency costs about the same: write-through store ack +
  flag poll + coherent reload ~2-3 us, and loads retire in order, so a poll cannot overtake the weight
  prefetch. Device-scope atomics serialize at ~14-20 ns per address. The only overlap that pays is
  dependency-free work riding along in an existing launch (QP).

== S1-A  cheaper stage-1 epilogue (packed SwiGLU, staged 16 B h / scale stores) ==
logs: r5_s1a.log; ATT att_r5/s1a_* (I=384 T=1024 hybrid2 BM64; I=1536 T=4096 pingpong); bench/s1_sweep.py
done earlier in round 5: packed v_pk_* SwiGLU (swiglu2, EF) and the il4 per-wave LDS-staged epilogue (S1S).
iterations (non-il4 pipes, row-major h; byte-identical h_q / h_s / out vs the 1 B path for hybrid2 BM16 / 64
/ 128, async BM32 and pingpong BM256 at I=384 T=300, I=1536 T=1000, I=768 T=4097):
  1    s1st: h bytes staged per wave in the freed LDS ring, stored as one 16 B row slice per lane (the HT
       path's staging, row-major addresses). Pingpong ATT: epilogue 9.5k -> 8.9k cycles/wave (byte stores
       64 -> 32, selects 95 -> 63, + ds_write_b8 + barrier); hybrid2 BM64 I=384 T=1024: the epilogue is 1.4%
       of wave time (loop 89%, MFMA duty 12.8%, gaps = buffer_load waits)
  2    + scale bytes staged and stored lane-per-row: pingpong epilogue 7.8k cycles/wave (-18% vs the 1 B
       path), median 8.0k -> 6.8k; what is left is SwiGLU / quant VALU (row-16 DPP max 128, min/max3 224,
       exp / rcp 128, cvt 32) and ds_write_b8 64. Stage 1 (s1_sweep, warm, 9 reps): I=384 T=256..4096
       +1.1 .. +2.9%, I=768 T=32 / 512 / 4096 +1.1 / +2.2 / +2.0%, I=1536 -0.9 .. +0.7%, T=32 BM16 -0..+0.1%
  3    s1sd: the staging in its own LDS past the ring (no barrier before the epilogue): pingpong +0.9 .. +2.7%
       (T=4096), but -1.9 .. -9.4% at small T (BM16: the extra LDS costs occupancy)
  gate fix: s1_sweep / st2_sweep compared HT runs' h through a row-major gather of the K-step-major planes
  (false rejects in HT cells, e.g. I=384 T=512, I=768 T=2048, I=1536 T=512 / 1024, even for no-op arms);
  compare.h_rows now returns row-major h for both layouts (HT and non-HT runs compare byte for byte).
verdict: SHIP s1st as a per-cell pick in make_tiles_v6 (non-HT, non-persistent stage 1; > +1.5% cells:
  I=384 T=2048 / 4096, I=768 T=512 / 4096), s1sd only with the pingpong pipe. The 171 us I=1536 epilogue
  target applied to the pingpong at large T, which il4 + S1S (S1-B / S1-C) replaced.
binding limit: at mid / small T stage 1 is bound by the weight-load latency (MFMA duty 13%, epilogue 1.4% of
  wave time), so the store path cannot move it; on the pingpong the epilogue is 8-10% of wave time and VALU-
  bound after the store fix (SwiGLU + e8m0 quant, ~220 VALU ops per 32 output values per lane).
